      "englishText": "Avadhānī: The leather bag in my hand proves that I am a cobbler. I also have a skin. So it is proved that I am a cobbler. I also like to search for new things like new words (note the pun on pāda, having the double meaning of 'words' as well as 'steps' and 'footprints'), new thoughts etc. Without it my work would not be possible. Therefore, I am obliged to you and I accept that I am a cobbler (carmakāra). \n\nNiṣiddhākṣarī: (playing with the word carmakāra) You are not a cobbler (carmakāra) but a person who creates wonders (camatkārakDra1). \n\n(He now continues from where the Avadhānī had been interrupted). 'La' is prohibited.\n\n(Anticipating that the Avadhānī would like to create the word 'a-ma-la' meaning pure, he prohibits the next syllable 'la'.)\n\nAvadhānī: 'rtya' (thus creating the word 'amartya' meaning immortal)\n\nNiṣiddhākṣarī: 'na' is forbidden (anticipating 'nda', which would form 'amartyDnanda' meaning 'the joy of immortality).\n\nAvadhānī: 'tma' (thus creating the word 'amartyātmā', meaning 'the immortal soul').\n\nNiṣiddhākṣarī: (unable to guess the word in the Avadhānī's mind, requests him) Please give the next syllable\n\nThe Bell: (sounds for the 1st time.)\n\nAvadhānī: (on hearing the soft sound of the bell, which was not loud enough): Please strike it a little more strongly!\n\n1The word camatkāra implies miracle, surprise, but also poetical charm — \nboth of which the Avadhani is supposed to show.",
