      "englishText": "Dattapadī: Respected Avadhānī, please compose a śloka in the praise of Lord Srinivasa, using a metre of your own choice (svacchandavṛttena), in which the words 'yatna', 'ratna', 'nutna', 'pratna' should occur.\n\nAprastutaprasanga: (playing with the word svacchanda): O, is there a metre like svacchanda?\t\n\t\t\nAvadhānī: Svacchanda4 is that metre, in which the syllables are in harmony with the poet, the time and the place. (He continues the composition for the Dattapadī): Yatnadeva...('with effort')\n\nAprastutaprasanga: When you said 'yatna' (effort) it came to my mind that I also do Ashtavadhana in Sanskrit as you do. But everyone calls you an Avadhānī, — nobody recognises me as such.\n\nAvadhānī: Ramakrishna, though himself a heap of problems, yet he does Avadhāna. Therefore I declare Ramakrishna to be an Avadhānī.\n\nCommentator: He is an Avadhānī (avadhanakrit5) in the sense that he disturbs the Avadhana (avadhanaam krintati by interfering repeatedly)\n\nAvadhānī: Here he disturbs (krintati) the Avadhāna, but at his place (in Tirupati) he is a Registrar. \n\nAprastutaprasanga: I am afraid that I may have to start the work of a Registrar at this moment.\n\nAvadhānī: (begins to compose the śloka as requested by the Dattapadī, using the word 'yatna'. The metre he uses is Śārdūlavikrīḍita, a nineteen-syllabled metre): \n\nYatnDdeva bhavat padDbjayugali sandarsanam no bhavet\n\n(May we behold Thy Lotus-feet through our effort)\n\nPresident: Here ends the first quarter of the Dattapadī-item.\n\n4Literally 'one's own way of behaviour'.\n 5The suffix 'krit' also can be formed from the root 'krint' which means 'cutting'. Here the commentator plays with the word 'krit' can be derived from both roots 'kri' = 'to do' and 'krint' = 'to cut'.",
