      "englishText": "Aprastutaprasanga: Yes, exactly. That time I was very hungry. So I wanted to have something to eat. Yet nothing eatable (tindi) could be found. Why was it so,— can you explain? \n\nAvadhānī: (to the audience): He saw Tindivanam. 'Tindi' in Telugu means food and 'vanam' means rich, good. The Aprastutaprasanga thought that this was a place where one could find good food. But 'vanam' means forest (in Sanskrit). So, actually that was a forest for eatable things meaning no food was available. By mistake he has gone to Tindivanam. (Turning to the Aprastutaprasanga) Really, you made a mistake by going to Tindivanam.\n\nNiṣiddhākṣarī:\t(expecting the word 'mahD'): 'ha' is forbidden.\n\nAvadhānī: 'nah'\n\nVyastākṣarī: the fourth syllable is 'na'.\n\nAvadhānī: Understood, 'na'.\n\nVyastākṣarī: The seventh syllable is 'mam'.\n(The Avadhānī repeats the same).\n\nNiṣiddhākṣarī: 'ka' is forbidden (anticipating the word 'manaskama' meaning desired by the mind)\n\nAvadhānī: 'sthi'\n\nNiṣiddhākṣarī: 'ta' is forbidden (expecting the word 'sthita' meaning stationed)\n\nAvadhānī: 'ram'. (Now the complete pāda of the Niṣiddhākṣarī reads):\n\nPresident: The second pāda of the Niṣiddhākṣarī is herewith completed.\n\nAvadhānī: (takes up next the second line of Samasyā and completes it in one stretch):\n\nbhDtDpyabhDtD prabalDshrunetre\n\n(It seems to appear and disappear in front of my eyes, full of tears)\n\nThe Bell: (sounds for the 10th time)\n\nVyastākṣarī: The eighth syllable is 'va'\t\n\nAvadhānī: 'va'\n\nAprastutaprasanga: O great Avadhānī, Kalidasa tells us that 'All that is old (purana)is not gold'. Which PurDna does he mean?14\n\nAvadhānī: He means the NavapurDna15, where it is discussed what is new and what is old, whether the new should be accepted or the old.\n\nVyastākṣarī: The first syllable is 'a'.\n\nAvadhānī: So it is 'a' (akDra)! In the Sanskrit alphabet the first syllable is 'a'.\n(Avadhānī continues immediately to complete the second line of the Dattapadī using the word 'ratna')\n\t\nratnDdhyam bhavadiyabhavyamukutam durDt kshanam sevyate\n(The beautiful crown on your head is enjoyed by us from afar)\nThe Bell: (sounds for the 11th time)\n\n14The word 'purana' can be used in the double sense of 1) the literal sense 'old', 2) indicating the scriptures called 'Puranas'.\n15Literaly, 'new purana'. Kalidasa has used the word 'purana' here in the primary sense of 'old'. He didn't refer to any Purana in his words. 'Navapurana' is a word coined by the Avadhani, meaning 'New Purana'. It does not exist among the scriptures known as Puranas. The Avadhani here plays on the suggestion thrown up by the questioner by combining both implicatios in his answer.",
